|> Exercise
    handle=_verbal_to_arithmetic_A_change_pt2_

    By what should the change mentioned in Exercise >>_verbal_to_arithmetic_A_change_
    be divided by in order 
    to obtain a difference quotient (cf. Exercise >>_difference_quotient_illustration_)?

    |> Solution

        One should divide by

        $$
        0.001
        $$

        since $11.2 = 11.199 + 0.001$. In more detail, we obtain the
        fraction 

        $$
        {A(11.2) - A(11.199) \over 0.001}
        $$

        after dividing by $0.001$,
        which is a difference
        quotient, having the form

        $$
        {f(x + h) - f(x) \over h}
        $$

        with $f = A$, $x = 11.199$, and $h = 0.001$. 

        |> StarDivider

        |> SolutionNote

            Very—_very_—anecdotally, one can observe that

            $$
            {A(11.199) - A(11.2) \over -0.001}
            $$

            is also a difference quotient, equal to the one
            above in value if not in syntax; in this case
            $$
            x = 11.2
            $$
            and
            $$
            h = -0.001
            $$
            compared to
            $$
            x = 11.199
            $$
            and
            $$
            h = 0.001
            $$
            above; what this underscores is that this diagram
            can be completed in either of two ways to illustrate
            a difference quotient, shown by the two diagrams
            immediately below:

            |> Image
                src=images/_e5_verbal_to_arithmetic_note1.svg

            !! ...can either be completed to illustrate
            !! a difference quotient like this...

            |> Image
                src=images/svg_ch4_newton_quotient_finished.svg

            !! ...or else like this...

            |> Image
                src=images/_e5_verbal_to_arithmetic_note3.svg

            !! ...with $x$ and $x + h$ interchanged, and with a sign
            !! change in $h$, computing the same slope of the same secant!
            In the last diagram $x$ and $x + h$
            are interchanged, and $h$ has changed signs,
            but the difference quotient gives
            the same slope of the same secant!
